  - [1mlocalStorage now, async-shaped API[0m — sync storage under a Promise-returning interface so a later server swap doesn't
    churn callers. Buys future flexibility; costs a loading state and async complexity in the form for a migration that
    may never happen. This is the YAGNI-questionable middle.

  Recommendation: [1mlocalStorage with a sync API.[0m It's the only option that works against this codebase as it exists, and
  the adapter boundary in the design will keep the swap cost contained if a backend ever lands. I'd skip the pre-emptive
  async shaping.

  One thing I'll bake in regardless: the password never goes to storage — only the username.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should preferences live? → localStorage, sync API (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: how the username gets remembered. This one changes both the stored schema and whether [38;5;153mindex.html[39m grows a
  control.

  - [1mOpt-in checkbox ("Remember me")[0m — schema holds [38;5;153mrememberUsername: boolean[39m + [38;5;153mlastUsername: string[39m. Nothing is written
    until the user asks for it, which is the right default on a login form that may run on a shared machine. Costs one
    checkbox in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and a little more wiring.
  - [1mAlways remember on successful login[0m — schema holds just [38;5;153mlastUsername[39m. No UI change, less code, but it silently
    persists an identifier the user never agreed to store, and there's no way to turn it off.

  Recommendation: [1mthe opt-in checkbox.[0m It's a login form; storing an identifier without consent is the kind of default
  that's awkward to walk back, and the checkbox is also the first real "preference" the store manages, which exercises
  the layer properly.
